/** @page execution_modes Execution Modes

    @tableofcontents

    @section exec_mode_intro Introduction

    %Qore supports multiple execution modes that control how code is executed at runtime. These modes
    offer different trade-offs between startup time, execution speed, and memory usage, allowing you
    to optimize for your specific use case.

    @section exec_mode_overview Execution Mode Overview

    |!Mode|!Description|!Best For
    |@ref exec_mode_ast "AST"|Traditional tree-walking interpreter|Legacy code, maximum compatibility
    |@ref exec_mode_ir "IR"|Bytecode interpreter|General use, good balance of startup and speed
    |@ref exec_mode_jit "JIT"|LLVM JIT compilation|CPU-intensive, long-running programs
    |@ref exec_mode_tiered "Tiered"|Adaptive compilation (default)|Production services, mixed workloads
    |@ref exec_mode_aot "AOT"|Ahead-of-time compilation|Deployment, IP protection, fastest startup

    @section exec_mode_ast AST Execution Mode

    The AST (Abstract Syntax Tree) mode is the traditional tree-walking interpreter that has been
    %Qore's execution engine since its inception. In this mode, the parsed AST is directly traversed
    and evaluated at runtime.

    <b>Characteristics:</b>
    - No compilation overhead at startup
    - Supports all %Qore features including non-@ref modern "%modern" code
    - Mature and well-tested
    - Lower execution speed compared to IR/JIT for compute-intensive code

    <b>When to use:</b>
    - Running legacy code that doesn't use @ref modern "%modern"
    - Short-lived scripts where startup time matters more than execution speed
    - Debugging scenarios where maximum compatibility is needed

    @code{.sh}
    qore --exec-mode=ast script.q
    @endcode

    @section exec_mode_ir IR Execution Mode

    The IR (Intermediate Representation) mode compiles %Qore code to a bytecode intermediate
    representation, which is then executed by a bytecode interpreter.

    <b>Characteristics:</b>
    - Faster execution than AST for most code
    - Low compilation overhead
    - Requires @ref modern "%modern" code (automatically falls back to AST for non-modern)
    - Good balance of startup time and execution speed

    <b>When to use:</b>
    - General-purpose scripting
    - Applications where both startup time and execution speed matter
    - Default choice for most use cases

    @code{.sh}
    qore --exec-mode=ir script.q
    @endcode

    @section exec_mode_jit JIT Execution Mode

    The JIT (Just-In-Time) mode compiles %Qore IR to native machine code using LLVM. This provides
    the fastest execution speed but has compilation overhead at startup.

    <b>Characteristics:</b>
    - Fastest execution speed for compute-intensive code
    - Higher startup time due to LLVM compilation
    - Requires @ref modern "%modern" code
    - Automatic fallback to IR for operations not yet natively implemented
    - Preserves all %Qore semantics including exception handling

    <b>Native optimizations include:</b>
    - Inline arithmetic fast-paths for int+int and float+float operations
    - Direct hash key access for constant string keys
    - Direct list index access with bounds checking
    - Specialized string concatenation
    - Inline comparison operations for common types

    <b>When to use:</b>
    - CPU-intensive algorithms and loops
    - Long-running services where compilation cost is amortized
    - Performance-critical applications

    @code{.sh}
    qore --exec-mode=jit script.q
    @endcode

    @subsection jit_debugging Debugging JIT-Compiled Code

    JIT-compiled %Qore code supports debugger integration via the GDB JIT interface. This allows
    debuggers like GDB and LLDB to see JIT-compiled function symbols and source locations.

    <b>Requirements:</b>
    - GDB 7.0 or later
    - LLDB with the jit-loader.gdb plugin enabled

    <b>Using GDB with JIT-compiled code:</b>

    To debug JIT-compiled code, set a pending breakpoint on the JIT registration function, then
    query for symbols after code is compiled:

    @code{.sh}
    $ gdb --args qore --exec-mode=jit script.q
    (gdb) set breakpoint pending on
    (gdb) break __jit_debug_register_code
    (gdb) run
    # GDB stops when JIT code is registered
    (gdb) continue
    # After program runs, query JIT symbols:
    (gdb) info functions _toplevel
    File script.q:
    6:    void _toplevel(void);
    @endcode

    JIT-compiled functions appear with their source file and line number, allowing you to set
    breakpoints and inspect JIT-compiled code just like native code.

    @note The GDB JIT interface registers symbols dynamically as code is compiled. Symbols become
    visible to the debugger after the \c __jit_debug_register_code function is called.

    @section exec_mode_tiered Tiered Execution Mode

    The tiered mode provides adaptive compilation by automatically promoting hot functions through
    execution tiers: AST → IR → JIT. Functions start on the AST interpreter and are promoted
    based on call frequency. This is the default execution mode in %Qore 2.3+.

    <b>Characteristics:</b>
    - Fast startup (starts with AST)
    - Hot code paths automatically optimized
    - Configurable promotion thresholds
    - Best of both worlds for mixed workloads

    <b>Promotion thresholds:</b>
    - \c --jit-ir-threshold=N: Calls before IR promotion (default: 100)
    - \c --jit-jit-threshold=N: Calls before JIT promotion (default: 1000)

    <b>When to use:</b>
    - Long-running services with varied workloads
    - Applications with both startup-sensitive and compute-intensive code paths
    - When you want automatic optimization without explicit mode selection

    @code{.sh}
    qore --exec-mode=tiered script.q
    qore --exec-mode=tiered --jit-ir-threshold=50 --jit-jit-threshold=500 script.q
    @endcode

    @section exec_mode_aot AOT Compilation

    AOT (Ahead-of-Time) compilation compiles %Qore scripts and modules to standalone native
    executables or binary modules before deployment. This eliminates parse-time overhead and
    can protect intellectual property.

    @subsection aot_scripts Compiling Scripts

    The \c --compile option compiles a %Qore script to a standalone ELF executable:

    @code{.sh}
    qore --compile script.q --output script
    ./script [args...]
    @endcode

    The resulting executable:
    - Links against \c libqore.so at runtime
    - Contains compiled native code for all functions
    - Includes embedded source by default (for error messages)
    - Supports all command-line arguments

    @subsection aot_modules Compiling Modules

    The \c --compile-module option compiles a %Qore module (\c .qm) to a binary module (\c .qmod):

    @code{.sh}
    qore --compile-module MyModule.qm
    # Creates MyModule.qmod
    @endcode

    Binary modules:
    - Load faster than source modules (no parse phase)
    - Are searched before source modules with the same base name
    - Support all module features

    @subsection aot_strip_source Source Stripping

    The \c --strip-source option removes source code from compiled binaries for IP protection:

    @code{.sh}
    qore --compile script.q --strip-source --output script
    qore --compile-module MyModule.qm --strip-source
    @endcode

    Source-stripped binaries:
    - Contain no readable source code
    - Use binary metadata serialization (QORD format)
    - Show \c "[AOT-compiled]" in stack traces
    - Cannot be decompiled

    @subsection aot_options AOT Options

    |!Option|!Description
    |\c --compile|Compile script to standalone executable
    |\c --compile-module|Compile module to binary module (\c .qmod)
    |\c --output=PATH|Specify output path
    |\c --strip-source|Remove source code from binary
    |\c --opt-level=N|LLVM optimization level (0-3, default: 2)
    |\c --static|Static link (requires \c libqore_static.a)
    |\c --target=TRIPLE|Cross-compilation target triple

    @section exec_mode_programmatic Programmatic Control

    The execution mode can be controlled programmatically using the @ref Qore::Program "Program" class:

    @code{.py}
    %modern

    # Create a program with JIT execution
    Program pgm(PO_MODERN);
    pgm.setExecMode(ExecMode::EM_JIT);
    pgm.parse("int sub compute() { return 42; }", "code");
    int result = pgm.callFunction("compute");

    # Query current execution mode
    ExecMode mode = pgm.getExecMode();
    if (mode == ExecMode::EM_JIT) {
        printf("Using JIT compilation\n");
    }
    @endcode

    @subsection exec_mode_enum ExecMode Enum

    The @ref Qore::ExecMode "ExecMode" enum provides type-safe execution mode values:

    |!Value|!Description
    |@ref Qore::ExecMode::EM_AST "EM_AST"|AST interpreter
    |@ref Qore::ExecMode::EM_IR "EM_IR"|IR bytecode interpreter
    |@ref Qore::ExecMode::EM_JIT "EM_JIT"|LLVM JIT compilation
    |@ref Qore::ExecMode::EM_TIERED "EM_TIERED"|Tiered adaptive compilation

    @subsection exec_mode_methods Program Methods

    - @ref Qore::Program::setExecMode() "Program::setExecMode(enum<ExecMode>)" - Set the execution mode
    - @ref Qore::Program::getExecMode() "Program::getExecMode()" - Get the current execution mode

    @note The execution mode should be set before parsing code. JIT and IR modes require
    @ref modern "%modern" parse options; non-modern code automatically falls back to AST.

    @section exec_mode_requirements Requirements

    @subsection exec_mode_llvm LLVM Requirement

    JIT and AOT compilation require LLVM 16 or later. Install the LLVM development packages:

    - <b>Debian/Ubuntu:</b> \c llvm-dev (or version-specific, e.g., \c llvm-20-dev)
    - <b>RHEL/Fedora:</b> \c llvm-devel
    - <b>Alpine:</b> \c llvm-dev

    @subsection exec_mode_modern Modern Code Requirement

    IR, JIT, and AOT modes require @ref modern "%modern" code. Non-modern code automatically
    falls back to AST execution. Use the @ref modern "%modern" parse directive or the \c .qr
    file extension to enable modern mode.

    @see
    - @ref Qore::Program "Program class"
    - @ref Qore::ExecMode "ExecMode enum"
    - @ref modern "%modern parse directive"
    - @ref command_line_processing "Command-line processing"
*/
